From 2f6897eddbf9a92f2e6367e9d9ab5870003122ca Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 21:16:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer: a seamless join of a Blu-ray played with its
 menus keeps the streams

With the disc's menus (navigation mode, HDMV or BD-J) every play item that libbluray opens was a held
event: the player drained its streams, closed the demuxer and the stream players and opened them again,
also where the playlist joins the two play items seamlessly. That is a pause in picture and sound at each
join of a film whose cuts are made of shared and specific clips (seamless branching), which plays without
one since RC6 when its playlist is played directly.

Now the next play item goes on without the player opening its streams again where all of this holds
(BlurayJoin.h, pure): libbluray tells it with the first bytes of its clip and they are where the play item
begins in the playlist (the read that opens a clip returns its first bytes; a jump - a seek, a menu's
command, a BD-J application - queues the play item before anything is read, so libbluray hands it on with
no bytes: read in bluray.c, _bd_read() and _read_ext()); its connection condition in the playlist, read
with Kodi's own parser, is 5 or 6; the play item before has no still; the streams that the playlist
presents in the two play items are the same (PID, coding, format, rate, language, aspect, sub-path,
dynamic range, colour space and flags; video, audio, PG, IG, secondary and Dolby Vision), and so are the
streams that the two clips carry (program map PID, every elementary stream with its attributes, the PCR
PID); the second view of a 3D disc is not read; and the demuxer reading puts the packets on the timeline
(SetTimelineReader(): the base view of a 3D disc does not). Anything else, or anything not known, plays
as before.

A play item kept goes on the timeline of the play item that the demuxer opened with, as the playlist
played directly does since RC6 (BlurayTitleTime.h): its distance from its IN time, plus where it starts in
the title less where that play item starts, on that play item's clock. The play items not kept keep the
clock of their clip, as before (the first of a demuxer, one after a still, one a seek landed in), and the
timeline of the joins after them is on it. The times of the disc's own graphics (IG) of a play item kept
are moved by the same amount.

Each join says in kodi.log what was done and why: "play item N (clip ...) follows play item N-1 of
playlist P seamlessly (connection condition 5) with the same streams: they go on, ..." or "... the player
opens its streams again for it (connection condition 1): its connection condition is not a seamless one
(5 or 6)". And each playlist of more than one play item says how many of its joins are seamless.

Run on the host with libbluray 1.5.0 and Hades' series playing a disc made for it from its HDMV First
Play object, and Read(), NextStream() and the new functions copied out of this file as they are: a
seamless join with the same streams goes on, its video and TrueHD on one timeline (0.000 s and -0.0004 s
at the join, where the clips' clocks step by +0.332 s), with nothing for the player to correct; a join
with connection condition 1 and a seamless one whose audio is on another PID open the streams again, as
before, and each new playlist does too. The tree of RC6 and three mutations of BlurayJoin.h fail the test.

Read, not seen on a disc: what the native Dolby Vision output does at a reopen. The renderer is not
configured again when the first picture of the new stream has the same parameters
(CRenderManager::Configure()), so native output is not ended by it; the reopen drains the old streams,
opens the decoder again (the enhancement layer is paired again from nothing) and waits for the two
streams to start in sync. A join kept does none of that.

Limits: the graphics composed from the first block of a play item kept are timed on the clock before
it (libbluray decodes them in the read that opens the clip, before the play item is told). Not tested: a
clip whose clock wraps before a join (the kept play item then follows the timestamps as libavformat
unwraps them).
